[2026-10-17 19:05] Task: authenticated webshells (objective 1.2).

What was done:
- Added `shellshock/creator/auth.py` (Creator-side, per the decision to keep emitted-PHP code out of the shared contract): `ALGORITHM = "sha256"`, `secret_hash(secret)` (hex digest), and `guard(vector, expected_hash)` which returns the PHP that reads the vector's credential and sets `$__ss_ok`. The credential channel per vector comes from `contract.AUTH_PARAM_NAMES` (cookie/post direct; header via the `HTTP_`+uppercase+underscore transform).
- `shellshock/creator/spec.py`: `validate()` now requires a secret for `auth` too (`mode in ("auth", "encrypted")`).
- `shellshock/creator/generator.py`: imports `auth`; `_context()` builds `auth_block` = `$__ss_ok = true;` for plain, or `auth.guard(vector, secret_hash(secret))` for auth (and, for the time being, encrypted, which behaves like auth until implemented - accepted as a temporary simplification).
- Templates (`cookie.php.tmpl`, `header.php.tmpl`, `post.php.tmpl`): added the `{{auth_block}}` placeholder and changed the gate to `if ($__ss_ok && $__ss_cmd !== "")`. The shell stays silent when the credential is missing or wrong (no oracle).
- `shellshock/cli.py`: `--mode` now accepts `plain` and `auth`; generation is wrapped so a `ValueError` (e.g. auth without `--secret`) prints a clean `error: ...` and exits 2 instead of a traceback. Updated the `--secret` help text.
- `shellsuite/protocol/contract.py`: refreshed the `AUTH_PARAM_NAMES` comment (no structural change).
- Tests: `tests/test_creator.py` (`test_auth_requires_secret`, `test_auth_embeds_hash_and_gate`, `test_plain_has_no_auth_material`, and `test_every_vector_is_php52_compatible` now covers plain+auth); `tests/test_shellshock_cli.py` (`test_create_auth_writes_gated_shell`, `test_create_auth_without_secret_fails_cleanly`).

What was tested:
- `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` -> 27 tests, all OK.
- Generated cookie/header/post auth shells by hand: each embeds the correct auth parameter, the SHA-256 digest of the password, and the `$__ss_ok` gate; plain shell has no auth material (`$__ss_ok = true;`).
- Digest checked against `sha256("hunter2")` = f52fbd32b2b3b86ff88ef6c490628285f482af15ddcb29541f94bcf526a3f6c7.

Notes:
- The password is never stored in plaintext: only its SHA-256 hash is embedded. `hash("sha256", ...)` is PHP 5.1.2+, so the output stays 5.2-compatible.
- Lab verification (curl with the correct credential executes; wrong/missing credential yields nothing) still to be done by hand after redeploying the regenerated files.
- Deferred on purpose (user decision): `encrypted` silently behaves like `auth` for now, and `spec.validate()` still accepts any `contract.MODES` value; both to be revisited when encryption (1.3) lands.


[2026-10-17 19:35] Task: refactor auth.py credential reading to a data-driven map.

Context:
- Review of `shellshock/creator/auth.py`: the per-vector read used an `if/elif/else` chain whose `else` assumed "header", so a new vector added to `contract.VECTORS` would silently generate wrong PHP.

What was done:
- Replaced the chain with a `_READERS` dict keyed by vector (cookie/header/post), each value a PHP statement using a `{name}` placeholder filled from `contract.AUTH_PARAM_NAMES`. `guard()` now looks the vector up and raises `ValueError` for one that has no reader, so a new contract vector fails loudly instead of falling into a wrong branch.
- `tests/test_creator.py`: added `test_guard_supports_every_contract_vector` (guard works for every contract vector) and `test_guard_rejects_unknown_vector`.

What was tested:
- `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` -> 29 tests, all OK.
- Generated PHP is unchanged for cookie/header/post.

Notes:
- Adding a future vector to the contract now needs one `_READERS` entry; the coverage test will fail until it is added.


[2026-10-17 20:20] Task: consolidate per-vector reading into one Creator module (Option 3).

Context:
- Two Creator sites encoded the per-vector PHP superglobal: the per-vector templates (command read) and `auth.py` (`_READERS`). Goal: one source for the vector->PHP-superglobal mapping, used by both the command and the credential.

What was done:
- Added `shellshock/creator/readers.py`: `_READERS[vector]` holds the PHP that assigns a channel value to a variable (`{var}`/`{name}` placeholders); `read(vector, var, name)` renders it and raises `ValueError` for an unknown vector; `assert_conformance()` raises `ValueError` unless `set(_READERS) == set(contract.VECTORS)`.
- `shellshock/creator/auth.py`: dropped its own `_READERS`; `guard()` now delegates to `readers.read(vector, "$__ss_auth", ...)`.
- `shellshock/creator/generator.py`: builds `cmd_read` from `readers.read(...)`; uses a single template; calls `readers.assert_conformance()` at the top of `generate()` so any drift fails loudly at generation time (the CLI already turns `ValueError` into a clean error + exit 2).
- Templates: replaced `cookie.php.tmpl` / `header.php.tmpl` / `post.php.tmpl` with a single `webshell.php.tmpl` using `{{cmd_read}}` + `{{auth_block}}` (deleted the three).
- `tests/test_creator.py`: added `test_readers_cover_contract_vectors`, `test_read_is_fully_rendered`, `test_assert_conformance_detects_mismatch`.

What was tested:
- `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` -> 32 tests, all OK.
- Manually generated all vectors x {plain, auth}: output is behavior-identical to before, with one cosmetic difference - in header/auth the temporary key variable is now `$__ss_key` (was `$__ss_akey`) because both reads share the lone reader; reassignment is harmless.

Notes:
- Runtime guards (outside tests, per request): `read()` raises for an unknown vector; `assert_conformance()` catches both missing and orphaned readers. No import-time check, to preserve the dispatcher's graceful skip on ImportError.
- Vector enumeration in the Creator went from two sites (templates + auth) to one (`readers._READERS`), guarded by the conformance test and `assert_conformance()`.
- `drivers.py` untouched (drivers are per-execution-function, not per-vector).
